>
  <p>
    Acne scarring happens when inflammation damages collagen in the dermis. Once that structural
    damage is done it does not fade with time, which is what separates a true scar from the brown
    or red marks that do.
  </p>

  <p>
    Telling those apart is the first job, and it changes everything that follows. A patient
    spending money on laser sessions for marks that would have faded by themselves is a common and
    avoidable outcome.
  </p>

  <h2>Types, because treatment differs</h2>

  <h3>Ice-pick scars</h3>
  <p>
    Narrow, deep and steep-sided, like a puncture. The hardest type to resurface, and usually
    treated with TCA CROSS — a focused application of acid into each scar — rather than lasers.
  </p>

  <h3>Boxcar scars</h3>
  <p>
    Wider depressions with sharply defined edges. Respond well to fractional resurfacing and
    microneedling radiofrequency.
  </p>

  <h3>Rolling scars</h3>
  <p>
    Broad, shallow undulations caused by fibrous bands tethering the skin to deeper tissue.
    Subcision — releasing those bands with a needle — is the key step, and resurfacing alone
    disappoints here.
  </p>

  <h3>Hypertrophic and keloid scars</h3>
  <p>
    Raised rather than depressed, more common on the chest, shoulders and jawline. Treated with
    intralesional steroid injections, sometimes with 5-FU. Resurfacing worsens them.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    Scars are examined under good lighting and from several angles, because depressions show
    differently under side lighting than straight on. Most patients have a mix of types, which is
    why plans usually combine techniques. Skin tone is assessed too — deeper skin carries a higher
    risk of post-procedure pigmentation, which changes device settings.
  </p>

  <p>
    If acne is still active, that is treated first. This is not negotiable.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatments</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Subcision</strong> — releases tethering bands under rolling scars. Often the highest-value single step</li>
    <li><strong>Microneedling radiofrequency (MNRF)</strong> — stimulates collagen with lower pigmentation risk in Indian skin than ablative lasers</li>
    <li><strong>Fractional CO2 laser</strong> — effective for boxcar scars and texture, with real downtime and a higher pigmentation risk that needs managing</li>
    <li><strong>TCA CROSS</strong> — for ice-pick scars specifically</li>
    <li><strong>Dermal fillers</strong> — lift individual depressed scars; temporary, lasting months to a year</li>
    <li><strong>Punch excision</strong> — for a few deep, isolated scars that will not resurface</li>
    <li><strong>Chemical peels</strong> — modest help for shallow texture; not a treatment for true scarring on their own</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>What determines the result more than the device</h3>
    <p>
      Sun protection between sessions, completing the planned course rather than stopping after
      two, and having the scar types correctly identified at the start. The same machine produces
      very different outcomes depending on those three.
    </p>
  </div>
